ts the version, and broadcasts\n\nFor the transform function itself, I'll implement it character-by-character:\n\nActually, let me just implement a clean OT system. I'll model operations as changes to the character array, and the transform function will handle all cases.\n\nHere's my simplified approach: I'll implement a `transform` function that takes two operations (applied to the same initial string) and returns `[op1', op2']` where the result is the same.\n\nFor deletes, I realize that a delete that overlaps with another delete should just reduce in size. And the positions need to be adjusted.\n\nLet me just code it up carefully. The key insight for delete-delete transform:\n\nWhen op1 = delete(p1, l1) and op2 = delete(p2, l2), we want:\n- op1' = delete(p1', l1') -- what op1 looks like after op2 has been applied\n- op2' = delete(p2', l2') -- what op2 looks like after op1 has been applied\n\nLet me compute this properly:\n\n```\nop1 = delete(p1, l1), op2 = delete(p2, l2)\np1_end = p1 + l1, p2_end = p2 + l2\n\n// How to compute op1' (op1 transformed against op2):\n// After op2 is applied, it removes [p2, p2_end) from the string.\n// Everything in op1's range that falls within [p2, p2_end) is already removed.\n// \n// The remaining characters from op1's range are:\n// - Characters before p2: [p1, min(p1_end, p2))  -- length = max(0, min(p1_end, p2) - p1)\n// - Characters after p2_end: [max(p1, p2_end), p1_end) -- length = max(0, p1_end - max(p1, p2_end))\n// \n// The position of the first part: p1 (if p1 < p2)\n// The position of the second part: p1 (if p1 > p2_end) or shift...\n// \n// If p1 < p2: first part stays at p1, second part shifts to p2 (because op2 compacts the string)\n// If p1 >= p2_end: everything shifts left by l2, so p1' = p1 - l2\n// If p1 is within [p2, p2_end): the first part vanishes, second part starts at p2\n\n// For the simplicity of implementation, I'll handle 5 cases:\n\n// Case 1: p1_end <= p2  → op1 entirely before op2\n//   op1' = op1 (u