e (the full content) after each operation. This avoids the need to transform for the sender.\n\nActually, the standard approach in collaborative editing is:\n- When server receives an operation from client A, it transforms it against any intervening operations, applies it, and broadcasts the TRANSFORMED operation to ALL clients (including A).\n- Client A receives the transformed version and applies it to their local state.\n\nIf we broadcast the raw delete(0, 2) to client A, and client A has already applied insert(1, 'X'), then:\n- A's local state: \"aXbc\"\n- A receives delete(0, 2)\n- If A applies delete(0, 2) naively: \"aXbc\" → \"bc\" (removes a and X) → WRONG\n- If A has already been told the transformed version: delete that skips X\n\nSo either:\n1. Broadcast atomic operations and have clients transform them (more complex)\n2. Broadcast the transformed version (the server does the work)\n3. Broadcast the full state instead of operations (simpler but more bandwidth)\n\nFor the benchmark, option 2 (server does transform for broadcast) is the standard approach. The server:\n1. Receives op from client A\n2. Transforms op against intervening ops → op'\n3. Applies op' to server state\n4. Broadcasts op' to all clients (including A)\n5. Client A receives op' and applies it\n\nFor op' that's just delete(0, 2), applying it on client A's state \"aXbc\" gives \"bc\". But the server state is \"Xc\". So client A and server would be out of sync.\n\nTHE REAL ISSUE is that client A already applied insert(1, 'X') locally. When they receive a delete(0, 2), they need to apply it to their local state. But their local state is \"aXbc\" where the server's state was \"abc\". The delete(0, 2) was relative to \"abc\", not \"aXbc\".\n\nIn the Jupiter model, each client tracks their version, and the server tracks the document version. When a client sends an operation, both the client and server know the version it was based on. The server transforms the operation, applies it, and broadca