+{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"3af90361-3017-4b0a-a29b-fb9a672fa141","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The problem is clear: the `applyOp` function requires the operation to consume the ENTIRE content string (idx must equal content.length at the end). But our `toOp` function for a delete operation like `delete(0,2)` on \"hello\" produces `[delete(2)]`, which consumes only 2 characters of the 5-character \"hello\" string.\n\nThe issue is the check at the end of `applyOp`:\n```\nif (idx !== content.length) {\n  throw new Error(`Operation didn't consume entire content...`);\n}\n```\n\nThis is because component-based operations represent the ENTIRE transformation from initial state to final state. A delete op should include a retain for the rest of the string after the deletion range. Similarly, an insert op should include a retain for the rest of the string after the insertion point.\n\nI need to fix `toOp` to append a \"retain the rest\" component at the end for any unprocessed characters. But since we don't know the content length at the time of constructing the op, we need a different approach.\n\nOptions:\n1. Require the content length when constructing ops (change `toOp` to take content length)\n2. In `applyOp`, don't require the entire content to be consumed (remove the check)\n3. Add a \"retain all remaining\" sentinel at the end of the op\n\nOption 2 is the simplest and most practical. For atomic operations like insert and delete, we don't need to consume the entire string — we just apply the change at the specified position. The component-based model within the OT module is separate from the atomic operations.\n\nLet me change the approach: `applyOp` should not require full consumption. It should only consume what the operation specifies and then append the rest.\n\nWait, but that breaks the semantics for transformed operations. When we have a [retain(2), delete(3), retain(5)] from the transform, we NEED the retain at the end to consume the rest.\n\nActually, the semantics should be: an operation that doesn't explicitly retain characters at the end implicitly drops them (doesn't include them in output). For a delete operation from an atomic op: `[delete(2)]` means \"delete the first 2 characters and keep the rest.\" For an insert: `[retain(2), insert('X')]` means \"keep first 2, insert X, keep the rest.\"\n\nBut that's exactly what the check enforces. The problem is that `toOp` for an atomic operation is only a partial representation. The fix is to make `applyOp` handle partial operations by appending the remaining content.\n\nLet me modify `applyOp` to not require `idx === content.length`. After processing all components, if there's remaining content, just append it.","signature":"3af90361-3017-4b0a-a29b-fb9a672fa141"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3148,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":73728,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3b5729a-6624-4d81-bb97-5a2094b26765","uuid":"ed5c426b-46ba-4b23-af97-5a3b6c23b07a"}
