d text. The issue is that B's retain(1) represents \"skip 1 character of the original string\" but it needs to represent \"skip the character that was originally at position 1 AND also skip the inserted X\" — in other words, it needs to retain 1 extra for the insertion.\n  \n  The correct approach: when A inserts and B retains:\n  - A's insert goes into the output\n  - B retains the length of A's insert (to skip over it), AND also retains its original content\n  - Since B's retain represents \"retain the original character at this position,\" we need to split B's retain into: retain(insert.length) for the inserted text + retain(original.count) for the original character\n  \n  But wait, another way to think about it. In the component model, insert vs retain should produce:\n  aOut: [insert(text)] for A's insert\n  bOut: [retain(text.length)] for B to skip over A's inserted text\n  \n  But this loses B's original retain! B's original retain(1) needs to also appear in the output, after the insertion.\n  \n  The correct output:\n  aOut: [insert('X')]\n  bOut: [retain(1), retain(1)]  ← no, that's two retains\n  \n  Actually, I think the issue is that in the standard OT component model, when A=insert and B=retain:\n  \n  After A inserts, B's retain should become retain that covers the insertion PLUS B's original retain.\n  \n  But the component-based transform should handle this. Let me look at how the standard OT transform works for components.\n  \n  The standard algorithm:\n  \n  ```\n  transform(a_components, b_components):\n    aOut = [], bOut = []\n    while a and b have components:\n      ca = a.next(), cb = b.next()\n      \n      if ca is retain(n) and cb is retain(m):\n        min_n = min(n, m)\n        aOut.add(retain(min_n))\n        bOut.add(retain(min_n))\n        decrease both n and m by min_n\n        \n      elif ca is retain(n) and cb is insert(text):\n        aOut.add(retain(text.length))\n        bOut.add(insert(text))\n        n -= text.length (decre