+{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"afb5ae6d-6cbf-4e94-baa6-3830e12ff7a5","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Still failing. The integration tests are timing out because the WebSocket connection doesn't receive the sync message. And there are unhandled errors about the database connection being closed when trying to remove connections.\n\nTwo issues:\n1. The WS connection isn't being established properly — the WS client connects but never gets a sync message\n2. The database is being closed before WebSocket cleanup handlers fire\n\nLet me investigate issue #1. The client uses `new WebSocket(wsUrl(doc.id))` where wsUrl returns `ws://localhost:${port}/document/${docId}`. The client constructs the URL and connects.\n\nBut on the server side, when `createApp` is called, the Express app doesn't listen directly — it needs the HTTP server. My `start` method creates the server properly. But the WS upgrade handler is on the HTTP server, not on the Express app.\n\nLet me debug by adding some console logging temporarily. Actually, let me first check if the basic server without WebSocket works. The REST test passes (1 passed), so the HTTP server works. The issue is with WebSocket connections.\n\nThe WS URL format: `ws://localhost:${port}/document/${docId}` — this should trigger the upgrade event on the HTTP server.\n\nLet me check if the issue is with how the client connects. Maybe the WebSocket from the `ws` library needs to wait for the server to be ready.\n\nActually, I think the issue might be simpler. Let me add error handling to see what's happening.\n\nLet me add a `console.log` to debug.\n\nActually, let me just look at the error more carefully. The error \"The database connection is not open\" happens during the cleanup after the test. And no sync message is received, meaning the WebSocket connection itself works (no error) but the sync message isn't being sent.\n\nWait — actually, the test creates the app and starts the server. The WS connects. But the WS listener for messages is set up AFTER the `open` event. So if the sync message arrives before the listener is registered...\n\nNo, I'm setting up the listener synchronously after `await new Promise(resolve => ws1.on('open', resolve))`. The `message` listener is set up before the sync message could arrive because JS is single-threaded.\n\nLet me try a different approach: set up the message listener before waiting for open.\n\nActually, the issue might be that the WS URL path is wrong. Let me check:\n- wsUrl(doc.id) returns `ws://localhost:${port}/document/${doc.id}`\n- The doc.id is a UUID\n- The server checks if `url.startsWith('/document/')`\n- The URL should be something like `/document/uuid`\n\nBut wait — the ws library might encode the URL differently. Let me check...\n\nActually, looking at the `http.IncomingMessage` object in the upgrade handler, `req.url` should be `/document/<id>`. That should match.\n\nLet me add a small debug test to see what's happening.\n\nWait, actually I think the issue might be something else. Let me check if it's a race condition — the WS connection opens before the server is ready.\n\nActually, looking at my test code again:\n```javascript\nport = await startServer();\n```\nThis returns the port after the server starts listening. Then:\n```javascript\nconst ws1 = new WebSocket(wsUrl(doc.id));\n```\nThis connects to the server. Then:\n```javascript\nawait new Promise((resolve) => ws1.on('open', resolve));\n```\nThis waits for the open event. Then:\n```javascript\nws1.on('message', (data) => { ... });\n```\nThis listens for messages. But the server might send the sync message immediately after the connection opens, before the test's message listener is attached!\n\nWait no, JavaScript is single-threaded and event-driven. The `open` event on the WebSocket resolves the promise. The promise `.then()` callback (or `await` continuation) adds the message listener in the next microtask. But the server's `connection` event fires, and the `send(sync)` call happens in the same macrotask as the `open` event. The `message` event on the client side would be queued for the next tick.\n\nActually, with ws library, the `open` event fires on the client when the server acknowledges the WS connection upgrade. At this point, the server has already set up the connection and may have already called `send(sync)`. The sync message data is buffered and would be delivered as a `message` event — but only if a listener is already registered.\n\nSo the issue IS a race condition: the server sends the sync message before the client registers its message listener.\n\nLet me fix this by registering the message listener BEFORE waiting for open:\n```javascript\nconst ws1 = new WebSocket(wsUrl(doc.id));\nconst ws1Messages = [];\nws1.on('message', (data) => ws1Messages.push(JSON.parse(data.toString())));\nawait new Promise((resolve) => ws1.on('open', resolve));\n```\n\nThis way, the message listener is registered before the WS connection even opens.\n\nLet me fix all the tests.","signature":"afb5ae6d-6cbf-4e94-baa6-3830e12ff7a5"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3150,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":122240,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3b5729a-6624-4d81-bb97-5a2094b26765","uuid":"e983266f-043f-4029-926e-2bbf24eb1a6f"}
